\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{clifford}
\tag{draft}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Finite blade coordinates refine Clifford multiplication}

\p{Retain the orthogonal #{R}-basis #{b:I\to M} and the inverse of #{2}
from the preceding theorem. Let #{\mathcal C} be the #{R}-module of finitely
supported coefficient families #{c=(c_s)} indexed by finite subsets of #{I},
and let #{\delta_s} be its standard coordinate vectors. Define a bilinear
product by the structure constants

##{\delta_s\ast\delta_t
=k(s,t)\delta_{s\mathbin{\triangle}t}.}

Equivalently, for every finite subset #{u},

##{(c\ast d)_u
=\sum_{\substack{s\in\operatorname{supp}(c),\ t\in\operatorname{supp}(d)\\
s\mathbin{\triangle}t=u}}
c_s d_t k(s,t).}

The sum and the resulting support are finite, even when #{I} is infinite.}

\p{Let

##{\eta(c)=\sum_s c_sE_s,\qquad
\operatorname{enc}=\eta^{-1}.}

The transported-basis identification in the preceding theorem makes #{\eta}
a linear equivalence, and its multiplication refinement is

##{\eta(c\ast d)=\eta(c)\eta(d),\qquad
\operatorname{enc}(xy)
=\operatorname{enc}(x)\ast\operatorname{enc}(y).}

To prove the first equality, expand the two finite sums, apply the blade
product to each pair, and regroup by symmetric difference. The second follows
by applying #{\eta^{-1}}. Thus the displayed structure constants, rather than
an implicit transport definition, determine the coordinate product.}

\p{For example, work over #{\mathbb Q} with ordered indices #{0<1<2} and
diagonal values #{(q_0,q_1,q_2)=(0,2,-3)}. The formula gives

##{\begin{aligned}
E_{\{0,2\}}E_{\{1,2\}}&=3E_{\{0,1\}},&
E_{\{1,2\}}E_{\{0,2\}}&=-3E_{\{0,1\}},\\
E_{\{0,1\}}E_{\{0,2\}}&=0,&
E_{\{1,2\}}^2&=6.
\end{aligned}}

The first product has one strict cross inversion, the second has two, and the
third retains the repeated radical factor #{q_0=0}. Also

##{(\delta_{\{0,2\}}+2\delta_{\{0,1\}})\ast\delta_{\{1,2\}}
=3\delta_{\{0,1\}}+4\delta_{\{0,2\}}.}

This matches
#{(E_{\{0,2\}}+2E_{\{0,1\}})E_{\{1,2\}}}. Nonzero basis blades remain
basis vectors although their products can vanish.}

\p{Orthogonality is essential to the symmetric-difference rule. Over
#{\mathbb Q}, take
#{Q(xe_0+ye_1)=x^2+xy+y^2}. Then #{B_Q(e_0,e_1)=1} and

##{T_Q(\iota_Q(e_0)\iota_Q(e_1))
=e_0\wedge e_1+\frac12.}

The correction is the half-polar value. The general construction in
\ref{fcap-0003} already gives #{T_Q} as a linear equivalence; this example
shows directly that it is not an algebra equivalence. The contraction and
change-of-form recurrences in
\citet{8.4, (8.54)--(8.55), p. 106}{wieser2024formalizing}
give the same sign and normalization.}

\p{The ascending-basis theorem and change-of-form maps in
\citet{Theorem 2, p. 3, and Theorems 34--35, pp. 55--56}{grinberg2016clifford}
corroborate the ordered basis and orthogonal transport. The latter is the
specialization with symmetric half-polar form #{B_Q/2}; an arbitrary
nonsymmetric form with the same diagonal need not define this transport.
Wieser's exterior equivalence likewise assumes an inverse of #{2}
\citet{9.3.5, p. 120}{wieser2024formalizing}.}

\p{The displayed finite formula is a mathematical coordinate refinement.
The currently public FCAP basis transport is noncomputable and does not expose
an executable coordinate type, multiplication kernel, or encode/decode
product theorem. Those declarations, together with any fixed finite-slot or
floating-point contract, remain a separate implementation step.}
